<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Given that <m>{\cos}\,x = {-\frac{56}{65}}</m> and <m>{90^\circ \lt x \lt 180^\circ}</m>, find the exact value of</p><div>(a) <m>{\sin}\,x =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/>  </div><div>(b) <m>\tan x =</m> <fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) Use <m>\sin^2 x + \cos^2 x = 1</m>: <m>{\sin}^2 x = 1 - \left({-\frac{56}{65}}\right)^2 = {\frac{1089}{4225}}</m>, so <m>{\sin}\,x = \pm\sqrt{{\frac{1089}{4225}}} = \pm {\frac{33}{65}}</m>.</p><p>For <m>{90^\circ \lt x \lt 180^\circ}</m>, <m>x</m> is in the second quadrant, where <m>{\sin}\,x</m> is positive. So <m>{\sin}\,x = {\frac{33}{65}}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>\tan x = \dfrac{\sin x}{\cos x} = {-\frac{33}{56}}</m>, which is negative in the second quadrant.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{33}{65}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>-\frac{33}{56}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>